WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
SELF OFFING RATE:
Since 2000, the self offing rate in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:12.060
States has increased by 50% from 11 per 100,000&nbsp;
people to 16 per 100,000 people. Many would blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:17.580
modern tech for much of this increase. People are&nbsp;
more glued to their phones and computers than ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.400
before leading to less human interactions and more&nbsp;
isolation resulting in high levels of depression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:30.360
and thus more self offings. Now, that could very&nbsp;
well be a factor but something that has a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:36.300
more concrete connection is a medication&nbsp;
called Fluoxetine or Prozac. Prozac is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:42.900
medication that is supposed to treat depression,&nbsp;
OCD, panic disorders, and bulimia. Theoretically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:48.420
it is supposed to accomplish this by increasing&nbsp;
Serotonin levels inside the brain which seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:54.240
have a positive correlation with mood, emotion,&nbsp;
and sleep. Today, Prozac is the 25th most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:00.840
prescribed medication with an estimated 23 million&nbsp;
patients within the US alone. So, at first glance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.640
Prozac seems like a miracle medication that has&nbsp;
helped hundreds of millions of people overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:12.240
the darkest part of their lives. But, what if I&nbsp;
told you that Prozac was actually doing the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:19.080
opposite? You see, while Prozac may be successful&nbsp;
at giving patients a surge of joy or excitement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.540
over the long term, it can actually make&nbsp;
patients more likely to end it all. Surely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.640
such a severe side effect was an unintended&nbsp;
consequence and something that Eli Lilly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.860
the creators of Prozac, simply didn’t discover&nbsp;
during their initial studies. Well actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.720
there’s reports from the British Medical Journal&nbsp;
suggesting that Eli Lilly not only knew about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.400
these side effects but that they went as far&nbsp;
as concealing the evidence that was suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.220
this. One of their own studies found that 38%&nbsp;
of patients taking Prozac displayed symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:56.220
of agitation and aggression while only 19% of&nbsp;
patients taking the placedo displayed the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:03.540
symptoms. And this was way back in 1988 but here’s&nbsp;
the catch. Eli Lilly allegedly never handed over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:09.000
these findings to the FDA clinical reviewer who&nbsp;
was responsible for approving the medication. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.360
this could’ve been an honest mistake but when&nbsp;
coincidence aligns with opportunity, that’s rarely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:22.560
the case. Not to mention, this is just one of Eli&nbsp;
Lilly’s many allegations and settlements. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.560
despite this shady history, Eli Lilly is not only&nbsp;
doing well, they’re doing phenomenal. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.320
their stock is up 5X within the past 5 years alone&nbsp;
which has given them a market cap of just over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:41.460
$400 billion. At this point, they’re often trading&nbsp;
spots with J&amp;J for the title of world’s largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:48.240
pharmaceutical company. So, here’s how Eli Lilly&nbsp;
managed to make $400 billion worth of blood money.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:58.440
ELI LILLY:
Taking a look back, the story of Eli Lilly dates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:05.100
back to the late 1800s to a man named Eli Lilly.&nbsp;
Eli was a Civil War veteran having served as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.380
colonel during the war. And he wanted to transfer&nbsp;
the altruistic ambitions of the Civil War into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.420
company of his own, only instead of fighting&nbsp;
slavery, he would be fighting disease. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:22.380
with that, Eli would open up a small laboratory in&nbsp;
Indianapolis in 1876. Eli wasn’t creating anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.540
ground breaking per se. He was simply producing&nbsp;
and selling medications that were already on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:33.180
market. But, thanks to his war legacy and a lack&nbsp;
of big pharma, it didn’t take Eli very long to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:38.400
get the people of Indianapolis behind him. One&nbsp;
of the first medicines that Eli sold was Quinine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.920
which is used to treat Malaria. This alone&nbsp;
allowed him to reach sales of of over $4000 by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:50.640
the end of the first year alone. That’s over a&nbsp;
$120,000 today. Within just 3 years though, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:57.420
would over 10X that amount growing to $49,000&nbsp;
in sales or over $1.3 million today. All of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:02.040
this extra revenue gave Eli plenty of headroom&nbsp;
to expand and invest back into the business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:08.700
and that’s exactly what he would do. In 1878, Eli&nbsp;
would hire his brother James as his first full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.680
time salesman, and James would start marketing&nbsp;
their products across the nation. Back at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.020
Eli was also investing heavily into research and&nbsp;
development. One of their first inventions was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:24.360
adding fruity flavors and sugar to their pills&nbsp;
which made them much more palatable and far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.880
more popular. As their medicines gained popularity&nbsp;
across the nation, Eli would go ahead and formally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:36.480
incorporate the company in 1881 and this marked&nbsp;
the beginning of Eli Lilly’s monumental rise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.540
Within the next 20 years, Eli Lilly would grow&nbsp;
to over 2000 product lines, more than $300,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:48.480
in annual sales, and nationwide relevance but&nbsp;
Eli never let this success get to his head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:54.540
He strongly believed that the industry needed&nbsp;
ethical reform. That miracle medicine needed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:00.540
replaced by medications that had strong scientific&nbsp;
backgrounds and mountains of evidence. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:06.660
the only types of medication that Eli carried up&nbsp;
until his death in 1898 and his son, Josiah, would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:12.900
uphold this legacy after he took over. During the&nbsp;
early 1900s, Eli Lilly would became masters of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:18.420
manufacturing which was really the secret behind&nbsp;
their extraordinary reach and scale. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:23.940
Eli Lilly was so good at manufacturing that they&nbsp;
would often have excess capacity that they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:30.000
sell off to their competitors. By 1917, they were&nbsp;
operating the largest capsule factory in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.180
producing more than 2.5 million capsules&nbsp;
per day. But with Eli’s son at the helm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:41.640
it seems that this success did get to his head&nbsp;
as it wasn’t long until we saw the first inklings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:47.280
of monopolistic anti consumer practices. And&nbsp;
this had to do with none other than Insulin.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.300
SHAKY FUNDAMENTALS:&nbsp;

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:59.220
In 1921, three scientists from the Univeristy&nbsp;
of Toronto began working on a treatment for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:05.100
diabetes which was at the time deadly. These guys&nbsp;
would end up developing a commercially producible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:11.040
insulin which ended up winning them a nobel prize.&nbsp;
Eli Lilly was watching all this unfold from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.320
sidelines and they were extremely interested&nbsp;
in the project from the very beginning. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.600
to their disappointment, the researchers were&nbsp;
extremely hesitant in regards to working with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.460
commercial firm. They had much more altruistic&nbsp;
motives and were more concerned about insulin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.800
helping people as opposed to generating a&nbsp;
profit. But, despite this, the researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:36.540
would eventually team up with Eli Lilly when the&nbsp;
realized that this was the only way to reach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:42.960
scale the dreamed of. And with that, Eli Lilly&nbsp;
began selling insulin in 1923 and they’ve had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:50.340
monopoly over insulin eversince. In fact, to this&nbsp;
day, Eli Lilly, Sanofi, and Novo Nordisk control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:57.180
90% of the US insulin market. For the first time,&nbsp;
Eli Lilly had a medication that people would buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.220
regardless of their financial situation and this&nbsp;
would quickly show during the Great Depression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:07.020
While people were losing their life savings,&nbsp;
companies were going bankrupt, and investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:13.380
were losing millions, Eli Lilly would actually&nbsp;
reach record high sales of $13 million in 1932.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:20.040
That’s the same as $287 million today. As you&nbsp;
can see, with insulin, Eli Lilly was starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:25.380
veer off from their altruistic roots but they had&nbsp;
yet to turn to the darkside completely. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.400
throughout the next few decades, they would&nbsp;
be involved in a few commendable efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.740
starting with WW2. During the war, Eli Lilly&nbsp;
would partner with the American Red Cross to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:40.200
process plasma for wounded soldiers. And by the&nbsp;
end of the war, they would process over 2 milllion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.820
pints of blood which was about 20% of all of the&nbsp;
plasma that was processed in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:51.360
But their contribution to WW2 was nothing in&nbsp;
comparison to their contribution Polio. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.940
paid attention in history class, the name Jonas&nbsp;
Salk may ring a bell. Salk is the legend that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:02.940
created the Polio vaccine. But not only did he&nbsp;
create the vaccine, but he also actively chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:08.100
to not patent it so that it could reach as&nbsp;
many people as possible. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:15.120
Salk is often described as a hero and rightfully&nbsp;
so but he didn’t do it alone. While he had created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.980
the vaccine, it still had to be distributed to&nbsp;
people around world and the first person to step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:26.400
up to the plate was of course Eli Lilly. But&nbsp;
this was also one of the last universally good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.560
activities that Eli would participate in. Since&nbsp;
then, most of their philanthropic activities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:37.740
have had an undertone of branding and marketing&nbsp;
which isn’t the worst thing but also not ideal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.660
But what has really given Eli a bad rep&nbsp;
since then is their medications themselves.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.500
THE DARK SIDE:
One of Eli’s first controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:58.860
medications was Darvon which came out in the&nbsp;
late 1950s. The medication was advertised as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:05.280
a mild pain killer which it was not very effective&nbsp;
at but what it was effective at was getting people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:11.400
addicted. And this is not too surprising when you&nbsp;
consider that it falls into the Opioid category.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:17.460
By the early 1970s, these dangerous side effects&nbsp;
were starting to become clear and non profits like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:22.740
Public Citizen were started to advocate for a ban.&nbsp;
Eli Lilly would of course try to defend Darvon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:29.340
claiming that it was safer and less addictive than&nbsp;
morphine. But the allegations claimed that Darvon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:34.080
was actually even less effective than Aspirin&nbsp;
in terms of pain relief but even more effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:40.980
than the white stuff in terms of killing people.&nbsp;
Despite this, it wasn’t till 2010 that the FDA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:46.980
would finally ban Darvon meaning that Eli Lilly&nbsp;
was able to profit off of Darvon for decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:52.800
At least, Darvon was just addictive, some of Eli’s&nbsp;
other medications were straight up harmful like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:59.760
an antibiotic called Erythromycin. In the 1970s,&nbsp;
evidence emerged that this antibiotic was toxic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:05.820
for the liver often resulting in Jaundice. The&nbsp;
FDA would initially ask Eli to discontinue the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:12.360
product but after another review at Eli’s request,&nbsp;
the FDA would somehow change the mind. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:19.080
Eli medication that was straight up deadly was the&nbsp;
synthetic estrogen DES. Apparently, the medication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:24.420
was supposed to prevent miscarriages but it&nbsp;
actually just led to women developing cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:30.240
Worse, it led to their daughters developing cancer&nbsp;
as well. A couple of class action lawsuits were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:35.160
filed against Eli but none of these really went&nbsp;
anywhere as Eli would just pay these people off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:41.220
by settling outside of court. You would think that&nbsp;
Eli would be more careful after these lawsuits but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:47.040
they would actually become more risk on with&nbsp;
an arthritis medication called Oraflex. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:53.700
early 1980s, dozens of people taking Oraflex would&nbsp;
end up dying due to liver and kidney failure. Any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:58.860
moral pharmaceutical company would pause the sale&nbsp;
of Oraflex and conduct more testing or atleast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:06.060
include a warning label. But, what Eli did was try&nbsp;
to cover up the fact that people were dying from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:12.780
regulators. And this is not an allegation, this&nbsp;
is actually a fact as Eli would end up pleading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:20.220
guilty. But, do you know what the fine was for&nbsp;
this heinous crime? A pitiful $25,000. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:24.840
we talk about how such fines are nothing in&nbsp;
comparison to what these companies earn. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:31.500
$25,000? I mean, that’s literally something that&nbsp;
a lot of everyday people can afford. Diabetes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:37.860
patients literally have to pay more than this to&nbsp;
Eli for insulin over a 5 year period. So, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:44.340
you can take that for what you will. All of this&nbsp;
brings us into Eli’s worst offense which is of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:50.940
course Prozac. In the late 1980s, Eli Lilly would&nbsp;
launch an anti depressent called Prozac despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:57.000
allegedly having evidence that Prozac increased&nbsp;
the likelihood of people self offing. When third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:02.880
party evidence came out against Prozac, Eli would&nbsp;
launch a full on campaign to defend Prozac. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:08.520
appears that Eli was successful at defending&nbsp;
Prozac but that also means that humanity was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:14.820
unsuccessful at defending our most vulnerable. And&nbsp;
the results speak for themselves. People ending it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:20.820
all has increased by 50% over the past 20 years&nbsp;
as hundreds millions of people took Prozac.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:29.700
THE TRUTH ABOUT ELI LILLY:
Since then, Eli Lilly has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:35.100
battled with hundreds of lawsuits and dozens of&nbsp;
fines. But while this has created some friction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:41.100
for their business, it has by no means stopped the&nbsp;
damage. None of this is to say that Eli Lilly is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:46.020
evil just because they put out medications&nbsp;
with side effects. The reality is that no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:52.260
clinical testing is perfect and some side effects&nbsp;
only show up at scale, so that’s not a problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:59.100
The reason that Eli Lilly is evil is because&nbsp;
they do not respond to this damage. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.960
they actively try to get away with it. They deny&nbsp;
that their medications are causing the damage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:08.160
they inist on keeping their medications&nbsp;
available despite mounting evidence against them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.980
and they even conceal evidence that makes&nbsp;
their medications look bad. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:20.220
they also exercise monopolistic control over much&nbsp;
of the pharmaceutical market all for what? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:24.780
to sell as many medications as possible&nbsp;
and to maximize profits over human lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:30.720
And looking at the numbers, this is one thing that&nbsp;
Eli is extraordinarily good at as they’re headed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:37.740
towards becoming a fortune 10 company. But, while&nbsp;
they may still have an empire, it doesn’t change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:43.380
that that empire was built on blood. Did you&nbsp;
know about Prozac or Eli Lilly’s role in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

